{
  "about": "Read by the two scans of git calls: tests/integration/test_git_env.py (the Python kinds) and scripts/__tests__/git-env.test.mjs (the JavaScript and TypeScript kinds). A text runs git when it is the word git alone or a path that ends in /git, or when it holds git followed by an option or by one of git's own commands (ending where no letter, digit or hyphen follows), at its start or after a space, a shell separator, a quote or a path's slash; a run-time part of a template or f-string is read as NUL and may be a command. Every such text counts wherever it sits, and passes only inside a call whose env names the helper. commands: git's own commands, the output of git --list-cmds=main (git 2.43.0). runs and names: texts the rule must take and must not take for a git command line. python_forms and node_forms: each form a git call takes, with {q} for a quote and {env} for the environment given or not; python_cases and node_cases: whole sources and what the scan finds in each (call: the last name of the innermost call around the text, null for none). not_run: each text that runs git in its words and is not run here, by file, the last name of the innermost call around it (null for none) and the text (null for every text in that call in that file), with the reason; each must still match a text. excuse_cases: per language, one excuse for every text of a call, a source, and what the scan still finds missing in it. runnable_cases: raw texts of runnable files of a kind no parser here reads, and those of them the rule takes for running git.",
